/*  abandonT -- A REFUSAL RAISED FROM AN EXPRESSION AT DEFINE TIME ABANDONS THE FILE,
    AND NOW SAYS SO. Built 2026-09-17 for fixIts F-76.

    ⚠ THIS FIXTURE TRUNCATES ON PURPOSE AND HAS NO REACHABLE stop(). That is the
    behaviour under test, not a defect in the file. It is safe under rule H5 because a
    truncating run is not a HANGING run -- it exits 0 promptly and cannot take the suite
    hostage.

    ⚠ ITS TERMINAL MARKER IS THE `ABANDONED` LINE ITSELF, and that is better than a print
    could be: the line is emitted from main() AFTER the parse returns, so it is
    UNREACHABLE except through the end of the run. H2 wants a sentinel that a truncated
    run cannot fake, and a truncation cannot fake this one.

    THE MECHANISM, measured 2026-09-17 with a probe at aCTionDefinE's tail:
      a refusal from a RULE ACTION during a definition reaches aCTionDefinE's
        refusalBoundary, is reported, and is CLEARED -- incant/pop/argRetiredT
      a refusal from an EXPRESSION during a definition produces NO tail line at all: the
        match fails before that action ever fires, so the boundary is not missed, it is
        STRUCTURALLY UNREACHABLE. Nothing clears the flag and the rest of the file dies.

    THE ROWS
      AB-1   the marker BEFORE the bad define runs -- pinned as a COUNT of exactly 1,
             because "the second one did not run" is an absence and H4 forbids one
      AB-2   the REFUSED line, by message
      AB-3   the ABANDONED line, by message, naming the file
    AB-1 at exactly 1 is what says the run stopped where it was supposed to: a 2 means
    the file was NOT abandoned and this whole fixture is measuring nothing.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(AbandonT);
define
    abRoot;
    abMark code={ cerr "AB-1 marker ran":; };
    ;
abMark(abRoot);
define
    abBad = abRoot.*abNope;
    ;
abMark(abRoot);
cerr "AB-UNREACHED -- if this prints, the run was NOT abandoned":;
stop();
